course: dictionary
lesson: 7532
title: Pray and prey: same sound, different meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pray is a verb, a doing word. It means to speak to God or a god, to give thanks, ask for help or say sorry. It can also mean to hope very strongly that something will happen. Pray, prays, prayed and praying are all forms of the same word.
explain: Use the context to choose the meaning. «We prayed for sunshine on sports day.» Nobody can make the sun shine by speaking, so prayed here means they hoped very strongly that it would be sunny.
explain: Watch out for prey, which sounds the same. «Prey» is an animal hunted for food. «Pray» means to speak to God or a god. The spelling is the clue: prey has an e, pray has an a.
explain: Pray often goes with other words: pray for, pray to, say a prayer. Many families pray before meals or at bedtime, and others do not pray. Both are respected, and you can read about how different faiths pray in religious education lessons.
fact: Pray is a verb: pray, prays, prayed, praying.
fact: «Prayed» is the past tense of pray.
fact: Prey and pray sound the same but mean different things, so check the spelling.
fact: «Mosque» is the Muslim place of prayer, where people pray side by side in rows.
fact: «Pray for» means to ask for something good for someone, or to hope very strongly for it.
exercise: «We prayed for sunshine on sports day.» What does prayed mean here? | Sang a happy song | Ran around the field very fast | *Hoped very strongly that something would happen | Asked the teacher for a drink
exercise: Which word means an animal hunted for food? | Pray | Pay | Play | *Prey
exercise: In a mosque, people pray side by side in rows. What does pray mean here? | Keep very quiet | Clean the floor together | Eat their lunch | *Speak to God as a way of worship
test: Which word fits the gap? «We ___ for rain so the garden would grow.» | *prayed | prey | paid | played
test: Which sentence uses pray to mean speak to God? | The lion will pray on the zebra | *Many people pray at bedtime | The fox went out to pray for its supper | Sam can pray a goal in football
exam: Which of these is the past tense of pray? | Prey | Prays | Praying | *Prayed
